{
  "dom_id": "step-q213601",
  "name": "Question 18",
  "problem": "Which of the following relations are antisymmetric? \n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x\\ge y,$ defined on $R$\n\n- $x\\,S\\,y$ if and only if $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5),$ defined on $Z$\n\n- $x\\,T\\,y$ if and only if $|x|=|y|,$ defined on $R$",
  "worked_solution": "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is antisymmetric. Indeed, if $(x,y)\\in R$ and $(y,x)\\in R,$ then $x=y.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx\\ge y\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ge x\\,⟹\\,x=y\n$$\n\nfor all $x,y\\in R.$\n\n- Relation $S$ is not antisymmetric. Notice that \n\n- $(1,6)\\in S$ since $1≡6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5),$ and\n\n- $(6,1)\\in S$ since $6≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5).$\n\n- Relation $T$ is not antisymmetric. Notice that \n\n- $(-1,1)\\in T$ since $|-1|=|1|,$ and\n\n- $(1,-1)\\in T$ since $|1|=|-1|.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$R$ only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
